Task 1: Set Up Required Repositories for IdM Installation
Subtask 1.1: Verify System Information
First, let's confirm we're working with the correct operating system and gather system information.

# Check RHEL version
cat /etc/redhat-release

# Verify system hostname
hostname

# Check available memory
free -h

# Check disk space
df -h
Subtask 1.2: Update System Packages
Ensure your system has the latest packages before proceeding with IdM installation.

# Update all system packages
sudo dnf update -y

# Clean package cache
sudo dnf clean all
Subtask 1.3: Enable Required Repositories
IdM packages are available in the standard RHEL repositories. Let's verify and enable the necessary repositories.

# List enabled repositories
sudo dnf repolist

# Enable AppStream repository (usually enabled by default)
sudo dnf config-manager --enable rhel-9-for-x86_64-appstream-rpms

# Enable BaseOS repository (usually enabled by default)
sudo dnf config-manager --enable rhel-9-for-x86_64-baseos-rpms
Task 2: Install Required Packages
Subtask 2.1: Install IdM Server Package Group
The IdM server installation includes all necessary components. We'll install the complete server package.

# Install IdM server with all components
sudo dnf install -y ipa-server ipa-server-dns

# Verify installation
rpm -qa | grep ipa-server
Subtask 2.2: Install Additional Required Packages
Install supporting packages that may be needed for full functionality.

# Install additional utilities
sudo dnf install -y bind-utils krb5-workstation

# Install firewall management tools
sudo dnf install -y firewalld

# Verify critical packages are installed
rpm -q ipa-server ipa-server-dns bind-utils krb5-workstation
Subtask 2.3: Verify Package Dependencies
Check that all required dependencies are properly installed.

# List IdM server package dependencies
dnf deplist ipa-server | head -20

# Check for any missing dependencies
dnf check
Task 3: Configure the IdM Server
Subtask 3.1: Prepare System Configuration
Before running the IdM server installation, we need to prepare the system configuration.

# Set a proper hostname (replace 'idm.example.com' with your desired hostname)
sudo hostnamectl set-hostname idm.example.com

# Verify hostname change
hostnamectl status

# Update /etc/hosts file
echo "$(ip route get 1.1.1.1 | awk '{print $7}' | head -1) idm.example.com idm" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts

# Verify hosts file
cat /etc/hosts
Subtask 3.2: Configure Firewall Rules
Configure the firewall to allow IdM services.

# Start and enable firewalld
sudo systemctl start firewalld
sudo systemctl enable firewalld

# Add IdM services to firewall
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=freeipa-ldap
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=freeipa-ldaps
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=dns
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ntp
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=http
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=https
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=kerberos
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=kpasswd

# Reload firewall configuration
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

# Verify firewall rules
sudo firewall-cmd --list-services
Subtask 3.3: Run IdM Server Installation
Now we'll run the interactive IdM server installation script.

# Run the IdM server installation with DNS
sudo ipa-server-install --setup-dns
Important: The installation script will prompt you for several configuration options. Here are the typical responses:

Server host name: Accept the default (idm.example.com) or enter your preferred FQDN
Domain name: Accept the default (example.com) or enter your domain
Realm name: Accept the default (EXAMPLE.COM) or enter your Kerberos realm
Directory Manager password: Enter a strong password (minimum 8 characters)
IPA admin password: Enter a strong password (minimum 8 characters)
DNS forwarders: Enter your DNS server IPs (e.g., 8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1) or press Enter for none
Reverse zone: Choose whether to configure reverse DNS
Continue with configuration: Type 'yes' to proceed
Subtask 3.4: Monitor Installation Progress
The installation process will take several minutes. Monitor the progress and watch for any errors.

# If you need to check installation logs during or after installation
sudo tail -f /var/log/ipaserver-install.log
Subtask 3.5: Verify IdM Server Installation
After successful installation, verify that all services are running properly.

# Check IdM service status
sudo ipactl status

# Verify Kerberos authentication
kinit admin

# Check if you can access IdM information
ipa user-show admin

# Test DNS resolution
nslookup idm.example.com

# Verify web interface accessibility
curl -k https://idm.example.com/ipa/ui/
Subtask 3.6: Configure IdM Client on the Server
Configure the server itself as an IdM client for full functionality.

# The server should already be configured as a client, but let's verify
ipa-client-install --unattended --principal admin --password 'YourAdminPassword'

# Verify client configuration
ipa config-show | grep "Default shell"
Task 4: Post-Installation Configuration and Testing
Subtask 4.1: Create Test User Account
Create a test user to verify IdM functionality.

# Create a test user
ipa user-add testuser --first=Test --last=User --email=testuser@example.com --password

# List all users
ipa user-find

# Show specific user details
ipa user-show testuser
Subtask 4.2: Test Web Interface Access
Verify that the IdM web interface is accessible and functional.

# Check if web services are running
sudo systemctl status httpd

# Test web interface connectivity
curl -I -k https://idm.example.com/ipa/ui/

# Display access information
echo "IdM Web Interface: https://$(hostname)/ipa/ui/"
echo "Username: admin"
echo "Use the admin password you set during installation"
Subtask 4.3: Verify DNS Functionality
Test the integrated DNS server functionality.

# Test DNS resolution for the IdM server
dig @localhost idm.example.com

# Test reverse DNS lookup
dig @localhost -x $(ip route get 1.1.1.1 | awk '{print $7}' | head -1)

# Check DNS service status
sudo systemctl status named-pkcs11
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Installation Fails Due to Hostname Resolution
Problem: Installation fails with hostname resolution errors.

Solution:

# Ensure hostname is properly set
sudo hostnamectl set-hostname idm.example.com

# Update /etc/hosts with correct IP and hostname
sudo vi /etc/hosts
# Add line: YOUR_IP_ADDRESS idm.example.com idm
Issue 2: Firewall Blocking Services
Problem: Services are not accessible due to firewall restrictions.

Solution:

# Temporarily disable firewall for testing
sudo systemctl stop firewalld

# Or add missing services
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=389/tcp
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=636/tcp
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=88/tcp
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=464/tcp
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
Issue 3: Services Not Starting
Problem: IdM services fail to start after installation.

Solution:

# Check service status
sudo ipactl status

# Restart all IdM services
sudo ipactl restart

# Check system logs for errors
sudo journalctl -u ipa -f
